parts.body:
Federal regulations define [[flood-definition|flood]] as a **general and temporary** condition of partial or complete inundation of normally dry land from:

- overflow of inland or tidal waters;
- unusual and rapid accumulation or runoff of surface waters from any source;
- mudflow (a river-like flow of liquid mud caused by flooding);

and it also includes collapse or subsidence of land along a shore caused by unusual waves, currents or water levels.

The Standard Flood Insurance Policy adds a size test: the inundation must affect **two or more acres** of normally dry land **or two or more properties** (one of which is the insured's).

Article 54 lets the association write:

- **Fire insurance**;
- **Extended coverage** — windstorm, hail, explosion, riot, riot attending a strike, civil commotion, aircraft, vehicles and smoke;
- **Broad form coverage** — extended coverage plus perils such as weight of ice, snow or sleet, falling objects, freezing, glass breakage, accidental discharge of water or steam, and collapse (authorized by statute for a limited period);
- **Additional perils coverage** written with those policies — vandalism and malicious mischief, sprinkler leakage, **rent insurance** (lost rental income when property is untenantable) and business interruption;
- **Homeowners insurance** — but only after the superintendent makes a **determination of necessity**, after a hearing, that meaningful coverage is unavailable in the voluntary market.

**Insurable property** is property the association finds insurable **after inspection** under criteria in its plan. Property can be refused if its ownership, condition, occupancy or maintenance characteristics violate public policy — but **neighborhood or area location shall not be considered**.
